From e91f261bffb0a017bc4116c361fffde48fc2b6f0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aryan Randeriya <aryan.k.randeriya@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 19:44:13 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 14/18] fix(layout): give an inline split around block children
 real geometry
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

An inline element whose children are blocks (Amazon's footer links: an
inline <a> holding an h5 and a display:block span) is spliced out of the
box tree so the blocks become children of the block container. That
splice left the inline with no box at all: getBoundingClientRect was
0x0 and getClientRects empty, so 25 footer links counted as invisible and
could not be clicked. Undecorated inline wrappers spliced next to a block
lost their geometry the same way.

CSS2 §9.2.1.1 splits such an inline around the blocks. Rebuild its
fragments after layout the way Chrome reports them:

- the blocks sit in an anonymous block box, exposed as one fragment of
  the inline: the containing block's content-box width, from the first
  block's top to the last block's bottom;
- an empty fragment at an edge where the inline starts or ends with a
  block;
- the inline's text before and after the blocks: the box-tree builder
  records every spliced inline, and a folded inline run now keeps those
  inlines open as edgeless owners around their members, so shaping
  yields their line fragments without changing line breaking.

getBoundingClientRect is the union of the non-empty fragments. Paint
still skips spliced inlines, as before. Hit testing inside the block
returns the block's content, whose ancestor chain includes the inline.

Amazon snapshot: 57 -> 83 visible links (Chrome 83). Live: amazon
53 -> 79, news.ycombinator.com 188 -> 226 (Chrome 226), wikipedia
905 -> 936; bing, duckduckgo and github unchanged.

